          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Is it ever correct to submit a code root without its decimal characters?",
          "answer": "**Yes, but only for codes that are already complete at three characters.** I10 (essential hypertension) is the example. The ICD-10-CM structure guide says a decimal point comes after the first three characters only \"when more follow,\" so I10 has no decimal because nothing follows it (*ICD-10-CM code structure and the conventions that govern selection*; *Coding hypertension: I10 and the hypertensive disease family*).\n\nMost codes are not complete at three characters. Those codes cannot be submitted as a root:\n\n- **E11 (type 2 diabetes)** is \"never used at three characters.\" Characters four and beyond name the complication, and the code with no complications ends in 9, such as E11.9 (*Coding type 2 diabetes: E11 and its complication subcategories*).\n- **J45 (asthma)** is \"never used at three characters.\" The remaining characters record severity and the state of the disease at the encounter (*Coding asthma: J45 and its severity and exacerbation axes*).\n\nSo the rule in these sources is: a three-character root may be submitted without a decimal only if it is a complete code. Whether a given code is complete depends on the code set and year.\n\nThe sources do not cover billing rules, payer requirements, or which code file was current for a particular claim. Code validity can change between years (*ICD-10-CM and WHO ICD-10*). For a real submission, check the current official code file and ask a certified medical coder.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
